1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,784
Head and flow. How much power is in your
stream?

2
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:09,023
A stream is a battery that keeps
refilling, as long as it rains.

3
00:00:09,203 --> 00:00:13,502
Water up the hill holds energy; gravity
releases it as it falls.

4
00:00:14,222 --> 00:00:15,675
Just two numbers matter.

5
00:00:15,855 --> 00:00:18,375
Head: how far the water drops, in metres.

6
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:20,002
And flow: litres per second.

7
00:00:20,722 --> 00:00:26,247
Power equals water's density, times
gravity, times flow, times head,

8
00:00:26,247 --> 00:00:27,629
times efficiency.

9
00:00:27,809 --> 00:00:29,951
Density and gravity never change.

10
00:00:30,131 --> 00:00:32,442
Your stream sets just two: flow and head.

11
00:00:33,162 --> 00:00:36,761
Take a stream: 5 litres a second, falling
10 metres.

12
00:00:36,941 --> 00:00:44,568
1,000 × 9.81 × 0.005 × 10: about 490
watts.

13
00:00:45,288 --> 00:00:48,460
But pipe, turbine and generator all lose
some.

14
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:53,058
At about 50% overall, that's roughly 245
watts.

15
00:00:53,778 --> 00:00:57,824
Small? It runs day and night: nearly 6
kilowatt hours a day.

16
00:00:58,004 --> 00:01:02,439
Enough for a satellite internet dish, all
day, with power to spare.

17
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:07,289
For a small stream, channel the whole flow
into a bucket, and time it.

18
00:01:07,469 --> 00:01:12,719
20 litres in 4 seconds is 5 litres a
second. Repeat, and average.

19
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:18,494
Measure after a dry spell, and design for
your lowest flow, not your best day.

20
00:01:19,214 --> 00:01:23,707
For head, take a clear hose full of water:
one end at the intake, one downhill.

21
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:28,823
Water sits level at both ends: measure the
drop, move down, repeat, and add them up.

22
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:32,536
Or fill a pipe from the intake, close the
end, and read a gauge:

23
00:01:32,536 --> 00:01:34,357
1 bar is about 10 metres of head.

24
00:01:35,077 --> 00:01:39,069
Streams rise fast and run cold, so never
measure alone.

25
00:01:39,249 --> 00:01:43,774
In the UK, diverting water to a turbine
almost always needs a licence,

26
00:01:43,774 --> 00:01:45,519
and the landowner's say-so.

27
00:01:46,239 --> 00:01:51,943
Remember: litres per second, times metres
of head, times five, gives you watts.

28
00:01:52,123 --> 00:01:56,525
5 × 10 × 5: about 250 watts.
